i was always  a firm believer of whatever gift that  you have culturally you should be able  to make a life out of it  right now i'm working on dog collars  um beaded dog collars i haven't made  money since march  so i got to figure out other ways to  take care of myself and my family so  what better way to do it than to uh use  your own culture  as the kovit 19 pandemic stretches into  a second year new orleans a city hit  hard  early on in the pandemic and whose  economy is dependent on tourism  is trying to keep case numbers down  while also figuring out what tourism  looks like  in 2021 to better understand how the  city's economic landscape is impacting  those whose livelihoods are connected to  the tourism industry  i spoke to a few people to find out how  they're surviving financially and what's  at stake for their businesses and their  communities  how important is tourism to the new  orleans economy tourism is our major  economic driver mardi gras is a big big  deal  there is a tremendous amount of tax  revenue generated so  we are certainly missing that the  destination marketing organization in  the city  says the tourism industry was down 70 in  2020 compared to 2019  but jay banks a city council member says  safety is the priority over finances  i clearly understand the economics of  this but i'm also clearly understanding  the health concerns of it  and while i would like to be able to say  that we could do everything we do and  keep people safe  unfortunately we can't  before the pandemic how how many nights  a week were you playing  so i was playing like two three clubs a  week okay  before the pandemic bernard henry who  was born and raised in treme  a historically black neighborhood with  deep-rooted musical traditions  comes from an important musical family  in new orleans chester jones is my  grandfather  and i have uncle bennett he's the leader  of the tram abrams band  and i have my brother corey here  henry says the gigs he's able to get now  